{"name":"Off White","hex":"#ffffe4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"off white","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative spelling of off-white."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of off-white."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/off_white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Off White","blocks":[{"text":"Off White is a 1979 album by American no wave band James White and the Blacks."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/32/James_White_and_the_Blacks_-_Off_White.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Off_White","wikidata":"Q7078935"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphi